WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.042
Use this slide to test whether you can explain the design decisions rather than only recognize the syntax.

00:00:07.042 --> 00:00:13.657
Why keep a natural identifier unique even when a surrogate primary key exists?

00:00:13.657 --> 00:00:17.767
Why does a many-to-many relationship need a junction table?

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:21.737
Which business rules belong in CHECK, FOREIGN KEY, or UNIQUE constraints?

00:00:21.737 --> 00:00:27.842
What kind of dependency does Second Normal Form address?

00:00:27.842 --> 00:00:28.422
What does Third Normal Form remove?

00:00:28.422 --> 00:00:34.597
Why might child line items cascade with a parent while master data is restricted?

00:00:34.597 --> 00:00:40.022
And why can a normalized operational source still feed a star schema?

00:00:40.022 --> 00:00:42.402
Try answering each in your own words.

00:00:42.402 --> 00:00:50.372
If you can explain the reason behind the design, the SQL implementation becomes much easier to remember and

00:00:50.372 --> 00:00:55.442
adapt. For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:55.442 --> 00:01:00.772
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:01:00.772 --> 00:01:05.302
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:01:05.302 --> 00:01:10.722
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:10.722 --> 00:01:17.787
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:17.787 --> 00:01:21.957
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:21.957 --> 00:01:26.662
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
